From 87d1552208975ee366f24666faf308d377af0874 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 03:38:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] Pay through blinded paths that start at this node, in the
 router

A blinded path whose introduction node is this node used to be refused
by the router with "introduction point as own node not supported", so a
payer that is the issuer's channel peer, the common case on a small
chain, could not pay a BOLT 11 invoice with blinded paths, nor a BOLT 12
invoice, whose paths are always blinded. The offer payer worked around
that by processing its own hop before handing the payment to the
router, which left BOLT 11 uncovered and disabled multi-part payments.

The router now does it for every payment. When a payment session is
started, each path that starts at this node has our hop processed: the
encrypted data is decrypted with the node's key, the next node is read
from it or looked up over the named channel, the next blinding point is
derived, and our relay fee and expiry are taken back out of the path's
aggregate so that the next hop receives at least what the recipient
computed and at most three millisatoshi plus a millionth more, which
the recipient keeps. A path that leads back to this node, or one whose
policy does not fit under the aggregate, is dropped; when no path is
left, the payment fails with a reason. QueryRoutes processes the same
way, so a queried route is the one a payment takes.

The offer payer's own processing is removed. Multi-part payments through
such paths work as through any other.

(cherry picked from commit 8af06fe0957c428c8e142a939be46afa354a821a)
